 ### 2.2.4 Bounded-Structure Incremental Updates and Their Theoretical Claims
 
-Zhang, Huang, Zhou, Guo, Li, Luo, Jiang, Fang, and Zhou \[4\] propose EraRAG, a hierarchical graph-based retrieval system designed for growing text corpora. Structurally, EraRAG organizes incoming data into size-bounded "buckets" using a hyperplane-based locality-sensitive hashing scheme; because bucket size is capped by a small constant, inserting new data only ever requires modifying a bounded, constant-sized region of the graph, regardless of how large the graph has already grown. The authors formally prove this property as their Theorem 4, establishing an amortized time complexity of O(Δ(nd \+ S\_LLM)) for inserting Δ new items — a bound that, if it holds, implies insertion cost should not increase merely because the pre-existing graph has grown larger.
+Zhang, Huang, Zhou, Guo, Li, Luo, Jiang, Fang, and Zhou \[4\] propose EraRAG, a hierarchical graph-based retrieval system designed for growing text corpora. Structurally, EraRAG organizes incoming data into size-bounded "buckets" using a hyperplane-based locality-sensitive hashing scheme; because bucket size is capped by a small constant, inserting new data only ever requires modifying a bounded, constant-sized region of the graph, regardless of how large the graph has already grown. The authors formally prove this property as their Theorem 4, establishing an amortized time complexity of O(Δ(b*d + S_LLM)) for inserting Δ new items, where d is the embedding dimension, b is the number of random hyperplanes (originally denoted n in \[4\], renamed here to avoid collision with graph size n, corresponding to Strategy D's n_bits), and S_LLM is the cost of language-model operations. In their formulation, b*d represents the CPU cost of computing hyperplane hash projections for the new items, while S_LLM represents the cost of re-summarizing affected text buckets across hierarchical layers. If this bound holds, it implies that insertion cost should not increase merely because the pre-existing graph has grown larger.
 
-This claim is of direct and central relevance to the present study for two reasons. First, it is, among all works reviewed, the only one to offer a formal proof of an amortized cost bound specifically for LLM-graph insertion, making it the strongest available theoretical anchor for this study's central research question. Second, and more importantly for motivating this study's contribution, a close reading of EraRAG's own reported experiments reveals that this bound is never empirically validated against the specific variable it claims to be independent of: total pre-existing graph size. Every dynamic-insertion experiment reported by the authors holds the pre-existing corpus at a fixed proportion (typically splitting a dataset into a 50% "initial" portion, with the remaining 50% inserted in fixed increments) and varies only the size of the *incoming batch*, never the size of the *graph being inserted into*. The proof itself — while broadly consistent with established amortized-analysis techniques such as those described in Section 2.1.2 — warrants closer scrutiny: the proof's central step — asserting that repeated bucket splits and merges will not cascade without formally invoking a standard amortized-analysis technique such as a potential-function or accounting argument — falls short of the rigor typically expected of such a bound. Importantly, because this study's experimental size-sweep protocol evaluates an un-amortised single insertion per trial after graph construction via setup(), update_s measures a single un-amortised step, and the empirical measurements do not evaluate EraRAG's multi-insertion amortisation claim.
